#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175746 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/id #uuid "ef520918-4e4b-516b-acaa-57a14b5c1405" :question/math-academy-id "e-16134" :question/problem "Calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx = \\big\\{ 6, \\: -2, \\: 7, \\: 1 \\big\\},\\qquad\ny = \\big\\{ 5, \\: -1, \\: 4, \\: 0 \\big\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ and $\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (6+(-2)+7+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (5+(-1)+4+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-3)(5-2)+(-2-3)(-1-2)+(7-3)(4-2)+(1-3)(0-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(3)(3)+(-5)(-3)+(4)(2)+(-2)(-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[9+15+8+4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 36 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175747 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "33adfe2b-297e-5c52-8c4f-088d0c2b66e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16136" :question/problem "| **Number of Hours of Sleep ($x$)** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Points Scored ($y$)** | $54$ | $68$ | $80$ | $90$ |\n\nA math teacher asked four of her students how many hours they had slept the night before an exam. The information she received is shown in the table above. Compute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overline{x}=5$ and $\\overline{y}=73.$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ and $\\overline{y} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n y_i$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-5)(54-73)+(4-5)(68-73)+(6-5)(80-73)+(8-5)(90-73)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(-19)+(-1)(-5)+(1)(7)+(3)(17)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[57+5+7+51] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9087cd44-503a-51c3-8f04-40fe65b14ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174269} {:answer/id #uuid "caaf81a9-a943-54a0-870a-d117dd946a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174270} {:answer/id #uuid "f621b923-d136-5bb2-93dd-bd0a11dcee65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174271} {:answer/id #uuid "00bb18e0-d159-5339-a1de-8278df118689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174272} {:answer/id #uuid "66cff592-58f2-5a82-be01-0cf2cda0a8b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caaf81a9-a943-54a0-870a-d117dd946a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174270} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9ddb89-9b77-521c-b63e-2dd998d851d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41c31e68-b6d7-5283-bb82-6cc7ac671a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-134079" :question/problem "| **Age, in years ($x$)** | $6$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Education, in years ($y$)** | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages and corresponding years of formal education completed by each of Mark's cousins.\nCompute the covariance of the data sets given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=11$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-11)(2-6)+(10-11)(4-6)+(12-11)(8-6)+(16-11)(10-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-5)(-4)+(-1)(-2)+(1)(2)+(5)(4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[20+2+2+20] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311e284a-a84b-5e7f-b5a1-e35c8f0d5168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266576} {:answer/id #uuid "64ccf930-ce1a-5735-bf79-470826f036dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267036} {:answer/id #uuid "76132fbf-5e71-5e70-90ec-a505fc2186fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267185} {:answer/id #uuid "853262fc-b92a-5438-baec-a53e6c209102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267317} {:answer/id #uuid "a31cd77b-6b2c-52dc-a626-7500d351a1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "311e284a-a84b-5e7f-b5a1-e35c8f0d5168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266576} :answer-field/id #uuid "aca651ef-8c84-5b8a-8875-73fe007986c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d0e8ef1-dbd3-5d5d-b9cb-f3092550c8dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-134155" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{4,\\,6,\\,8,\\,2\\},\\,y=\\{2,\\,4,\\,10,\\,8\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=6.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-5)(2-6)+(6-5)(4-6)+(8-5)(10-6)+(2-5)(8-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(-4)+(1)(-2)+(3)(4)+(-3)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4-2+12-6] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9c6ba6-69a9-5ee8-9708-70c11ce21cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501547} {:answer/id #uuid "31a13e4e-c027-5991-a7d1-412a4ff0c00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501556} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2b6eb8-c4d1-56b2-89dc-4001ee07443f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501581} {:answer/id #uuid "74ac785e-85e2-5e6e-bc31-bcc9001443e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501681} {:answer/id #uuid "decfb95f-7b91-5304-b64a-c323fdf22255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d9c6ba6-69a9-5ee8-9708-70c11ce21cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501547} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa3053ff-8bf6-5a5f-a48f-e812f51a8989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502034}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3623d58e-6308-55c7-b844-a67fd7315f21" :question/math-academy-id "q-134167" :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{1,\\,4,\\,5,\\,6\\},\\,y=\\{4,\\,5,\\,2,\\,1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1+4+5+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+5+2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(1-4)(4-3)+(4-4)(5-3)+(5-4)(2-3)+(6-4)(1-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-3+0-1-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1499dc15-38f5-5d95-918a-72ffb6809888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272571} {:answer/id #uuid "762be50a-18a9-51c8-a54f-ca9f26009a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273392} {:answer/id #uuid "94928e78-723e-558e-8935-de7f73fdfbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273681} {:answer/id #uuid "d1650319-7a64-53ee-ad96-ad0f30650ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274213} {:answer/id #uuid "e3444b21-54ff-574a-baf1-e04c118fe0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "762be50a-18a9-51c8-a54f-ca9f26009a41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273392} :answer-field/id #uuid "988def41-f2b9-537a-bb4c-556b664be748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66fe2369-0f88-574b-b368-3759760d7d2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-149616" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{12,\\,7,\\,6,\\,3\\},\\,y=\\{-4,\\,-6,\\,-4,\\,-2\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=7$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=-4.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(12-7)(-4-(-4))+(7-7)(-6-(-4)) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(6-7)(-4-(-4))+(3-7)(-2-(-4))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(5)(0)+(0)(-2)+(-1)(0)+(-4)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+0+0-8] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f5e9ece-9a87-5054-8ed0-0e3968f7e786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273208} {:answer/id #uuid "7be7f640-87ec-538f-b7cb-1d5c9ca6d396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273451} {:answer/id #uuid "86a68a8c-c5d0-52d8-b452-f9cd176c9a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273541} {:answer/id #uuid "aa92d71f-4404-50a8-ac36-286273fb47d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273860} {:answer/id #uuid "e345be0c-e480-5b52-9594-53531742421e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7be7f640-87ec-538f-b7cb-1d5c9ca6d396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273451} :answer-field/id #uuid "8961d711-317f-5070-bbb5-79330b1171d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9c47870-1c3c-5cbd-a10d-e30d2edb18f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-149617" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{6,\\,4,\\,8,\\,6\\},\\,y=\\{4,\\,7,\\,11,\\,14\\}\n$$\n\nYou're given that $\\overset{―}{x}=6$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=9.$ Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-6)(4-9)+(4-6)(7-9)+(8-6)(11-9)+(6-6)(14-9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(0)(-5)+(-2)(-2)+(2)(2)+(0)(5)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[0+4+4+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
